WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.060
Graft a second notochord against the side of a chick neural tube.

2
00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:10.940
The neural cells beside it start to behave like floor plate.

3
00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:13.790
The tube gains a second bottom.

4
00:00:14.710 --> 00:00:17.490
So how does a cell in the wall know how far it sits from the bottom?

5
00:00:18.533 --> 00:00:20.053
Its neighbours tell it.

6
00:00:20.633 --> 00:00:24.653
A cell's position is written in how much signal it receives from below and above.

7
00:00:25.717 --> 00:00:26.777
Start with the map.

8
00:00:27.677 --> 00:00:31.757
The neural tube rolls up from the neural plate and becomes the brain and spinal

9
00:00:31.757 --> 00:00:32.757
cord.

10
00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:38.150
Under the tube runs the notochord, a rod of mesoderm, the embryo's middle cell

11
00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:39.150
layer.

12
00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:43.820
The tube's bottom strip is the floor plate, a narrow line of specialized cells.

13
00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:46.260
Its top strip is the roof plate.

14
00:00:47.233 --> 00:00:51.493
The epidermis, the non-neural ectoderm, ends up over the top of the tube.

15
00:00:52.467 --> 00:00:57.147
In the wall between floor and roof, dividing cells called progenitors line the

16
00:00:57.147 --> 00:00:58.147
central canal.

17
00:00:58.817 --> 00:01:03.477
They will make motor neurons near the bottom, and relay neurons for body sensation

18
00:01:03.477 --> 00:01:04.477
near the top.

19
00:01:05.267 --> 00:01:09.667
The ventral half of the wall will hold five stripes of progenitors, called domains.

20
00:01:10.767 --> 00:01:12.207
Each makes one class of neuron.

21
00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:14.880
The map raises four puzzles.

22
00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:16.880
Who marks the bottom?

23
00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:18.600
What is the signal?

24
00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:20.380
How is a slope read?

25
00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:22.540
How does a slope become lines?

26
00:01:23.633 --> 00:01:24.813
Start with the first.

27
00:01:25.833 --> 00:01:28.133
Notochord and floor plate touch and share features.

28
00:01:29.013 --> 00:01:30.953
Is that a shared history, or an instruction?

29
00:01:32.083 --> 00:01:37.643
In 1990, Marysia Placzek, with Thomas Jessell, Jane Dodd and colleagues, ran the

30
00:01:37.643 --> 00:01:38.803
test in chick embryos.

31
00:01:40.017 --> 00:01:44.977
A notochord grafted beside the side wall gave the nearby neural cells floor-plate

32
00:01:44.977 --> 00:01:45.977
properties.

33
00:01:46.517 --> 00:01:51.817
Now the reverse. Predict: what happens at the midline if you remove the normal

34
00:01:51.817 --> 00:01:52.817
notochord early?

35
00:01:59.150 --> 00:02:01.990
Floor-plate properties failed to appear at the midline.

36
00:02:02.950 --> 00:02:05.150
The graft shows the notochord is sufficient.

37
00:02:06.070 --> 00:02:08.670
It is enough to give floor-plate properties in a new place.

38
00:02:09.683 --> 00:02:11.623
The removal shows it is necessary.

39
00:02:12.663 --> 00:02:14.783
Without it, the normal floor plate does not form.

40
00:02:15.783 --> 00:02:18.063
Then the floor plate passes the message on.

41
00:02:18.903 --> 00:02:23.143
In culture, floor plate induced floor-plate markers, just as notochord did.

42
00:02:24.117 --> 00:02:25.997
The signals acted by contact.

43
00:02:26.897 --> 00:02:31.477
In this homeogenetic induction, an induced tissue recruits its neighbours to the

44
00:02:31.477 --> 00:02:32.477
same identity.

45
00:02:33.217 --> 00:02:35.757
Keep one idea in view through every chapter.

46
00:02:36.677 --> 00:02:37.897
The neighbours supply a slope.

47
00:02:38.737 --> 00:02:40.117
The progenitors draw the lines.

48
00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:46.130
The takeaway: in these assays, the bottom of the tube is told, and once told, it

49
00:02:46.130 --> 00:02:49.010
recruits. Something passes from the notochord.

50
00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:50.550
What is it?
